{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqqt68limis","workspace":"D:\\zonoid","title":"fair agentic multi round search loses to single shot for code symbol lookup","summary":"Airtight benchmark (fresh isolated daemon per 3-query batch to dodge a daemon wedge; lib+routes 1174 enriched code_nodes; 13-query locator corpus): multi-round agentic context search (runAgenticContextSearches via POST /subconscious/search-context) scores macro recall 0.346, 5/13 hits, 676 tokens per correct symbol — LESS THAN HALF of single-shot enriched GET /search (0.705, 10/13, 442 tok/correct) and below even shipped thin single-shot (0.385). This CORRECTS the earlier confounded conclusion (note:note-mqqpqf49a5m claimed agentic \"structurally cannot select code_nodes\" — that was a probe-task self-match artifact of driving the search via the assignment endpoint, where the anchor task summary equalled the query). With a neutral anchor task plus the raw query, agentic DOES select code_nodes. Failure mechanism: the confidence gate ABSTAINS on 7 of 13 queries (stop_reason insufficient_evidence_quality, selects nothing, recall 0); drifts to thematically-adjacent-wrong symbols on 1 of 13 (q02 picked note/guidance symbols for a classifyNoteType/externalGap/gateTask query); but when it commits (confidence_high) it hits 5 of 6. Abstention is the killer: agentic is tuned for precision and avoiding false context (good for agent workflows), the opposite of what a recall locator benchmark rewards. For exact symbol lookup, single-shot top-k is strictly better and cheaper. SEPARATE PRODUCTION BUG observed: POST /subconscious/search-context wedges the daemon event loop after about 3 back-to-back calls (/health times out; reproduced twice on isolated bench daemons), worked around via fresh-daemon-per-batch.","category":"project","tags":[],"created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-23T15:37:51.366Z","vec":[-0.0681208074092865,-0.0383060984313488,-0.032952167093753815,0.032611679285764694,0.06762474030256271,-0.03719